WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.891
- [Instructor] Nth derivative
of g at x equals zero is given

00:03.891 --> 00:08.075
by, so the nth derivative
of g evaluated x equals zero

00:08.075 --> 00:10.194
is equal to square root of n
plus seven over n to the third

00:10.194 --> 00:12.699
for n is greater than or equal to one.

00:12.699 --> 00:15.890
What is the coefficient for
the term containing x squared

00:15.890 --> 00:18.307
in the Maclaurin series of g?

00:19.150 --> 00:23.450
Let's just think about the
Maclaurin series for g.

00:23.450 --> 00:26.729
If I were to have my function g of x.

00:26.729 --> 00:29.583
The Maclaurin series, I could
say approximately equal to

00:29.583 --> 00:31.938
especially if I'm not gonna
list out all of the terms,

00:31.938 --> 00:35.630
is going to be equal to,
well it's going to be equal

00:35.630 --> 00:39.047
to g of zero plus g prime of zero times x

00:43.789 --> 00:46.956
plus g prime prime of zero divided by,

00:48.854 --> 00:51.489
I could say two factorial
but that's just two,

00:51.489 --> 00:54.416
times x squared, and that's
about as far as we go.

00:54.416 --> 00:56.259
Because we just have to think
about what is the coefficient

00:56.259 --> 00:57.972
for the term containing x squared.

00:57.972 --> 00:59.680
If they said what's the coefficient

00:59.680 --> 01:01.865
for the term containing x to
the third, I would keep going.

01:01.865 --> 01:02.934
I'd go g prime.

01:02.934 --> 01:05.512
I would take the third
derivative evaluated at zero

01:05.512 --> 01:07.151
over three factorial.

01:07.151 --> 01:08.504
I could do this as a factorial too,

01:08.504 --> 01:10.027
but that just evaluates to two.

01:10.027 --> 01:12.042
I could do this as one factorial.

01:12.042 --> 01:14.849
I could do this as zero
factorial just so you see

01:14.849 --> 01:17.101
it's a consistent idea here.

01:17.101 --> 01:20.831
I could, of course, keep on
going, but we just care about,

01:20.831 --> 01:22.491
they're just asking us
what is the coefficient

01:22.491 --> 01:25.436
for the term containing x squared?

01:25.436 --> 01:27.831
They just want us to figure out this.

01:27.831 --> 01:29.845
What is this thing right over here?

01:29.845 --> 01:31.217
To know that, we need to figure out

01:31.217 --> 01:35.408
what is the second derivative
of g evaluated x equals zero?

01:35.408 --> 01:36.539
Well they tell us that over here.

01:36.539 --> 01:38.474
It's a little bit unconventional

01:38.474 --> 01:41.814
where they give us a
formula, a general formula

01:41.814 --> 01:44.586
for any derivative
evaluated x equals zero,

01:44.586 --> 01:45.674
but that's what they're telling us here.

01:45.674 --> 01:48.596
So in this case, the n isn't zero.

01:48.596 --> 01:50.717
The n is the derivative we're
taking, and that's going

01:50.717 --> 01:54.166
to be our second derivative, so this is,

01:54.166 --> 01:56.749
so if I wanted to figure out g.

01:57.645 --> 02:01.896
If I am figuring out
the second derivative,

02:01.896 --> 02:04.462
and I could write it
like that evaluated zero,

02:04.462 --> 02:06.304
or I could write it like this

02:06.304 --> 02:08.582
just so the notation is consistent.

02:08.582 --> 02:10.549
I could write it like that.

02:10.549 --> 02:14.383
The second derivative evaluated
x equals zero is going

02:14.383 --> 02:16.984
to be equal to, well our n is two,

02:16.984 --> 02:18.868
so this is going to be the square root

02:18.868 --> 02:22.701
of two plus seven over
two to the third power.

02:24.217 --> 02:27.668
Two plus seven is nine, take
the principle root of that.

02:27.668 --> 02:30.505
It's gonna give us positive
three over two to the third

02:30.505 --> 02:34.477
which is eight, so this
part right over here is 3/8,

02:34.477 --> 02:38.339
so the whole coefficient
is going to be 3/8,

02:38.339 --> 02:42.901
that's this numerator,
divided by, divided by two.

02:42.901 --> 02:47.420
Which of course is equal
to 3/16, and we're done.

02:47.420 --> 02:50.052
They didn't want us to
figure out a couple of terms

02:50.052 --> 02:52.810
of this which we could call
the Maclaurin polynomial

02:52.810 --> 02:54.427
and nth degree Maclaurin polynomial.

02:54.427 --> 02:55.973
They didn't want us to
find the entire, you know,

02:55.973 --> 02:57.769
keep going with this series.

02:57.769 --> 02:59.771
They just wanted to find
one coefficient right here.

02:59.771 --> 03:02.276
The coefficient on the second degree term

03:02.276 --> 03:04.873
which we just figured out is 3/16.
